WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.469
A safe design starts by identifying who or what can influence each part of
a process execution request.

2
00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:17.744
User-controlled input may appear as form fields, API parameters, file
names, uploaded files, task identifiers, database-stored parameters,
configuration values, queue messages, scheduled job inputs, or third-party
data.

3
00:00:17.744 --> 00:00:25.884
Some values look internal because they came from a database or queue, but
they may have originated from a user or external system earlier in the
workflow.

4
00:00:25.884 --> 00:00:29.700
Different pieces of an execution request have different sensitivity.

5
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.625
The executable name or path decides what program runs.

6
00:00:32.625 --> 00:00:34.788
Arguments decide what the program does.

7
00:00:34.788 --> 00:00:39.112
The working directory can change which files are visible by relative
reference.

8
00:00:39.112 --> 00:00:42.483
Environment variables can change behavior or expose secrets.

9
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:46.617
Input files and output locations can affect confidentiality and integrity.

10
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:49.415
Timeouts and resource limits affect availability.

11
00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.895
Logs and error handling affect auditability.

12
00:00:51.895 --> 00:00:59.463
Untrusted data should not control executable names, shell syntax, command
structure, privileged options, or sensitive target locations.

13
00:00:59.463 --> 00:01:05.060
The application should map a user intent to a small set of internal
operations that are already approved.

14
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:11.483
For example, a user may choose a report format or processing mode, while
the code maps that choice to known safe behavior.

15
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:15.617
The user should not be writing the command line through an application
field.

16
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:19.687
Trust-boundary documentation makes review and incident response easier.

17
00:01:19.687 --> 00:01:33.042
A reviewer should be able to see which inputs are trusted, which are
validated, which are constrained by allowlists, which files may be
touched, which account runs the process, which environment variables are
passed, and which outputs are captured.

18
00:01:33.042 --> 00:01:39.720
Without that map, process execution tends to grow through convenience
until nobody knows which parts are user-influenced.
